-- title:
--   Lebesgue differentiation along the shrinking lattice tiling
-- statement:
--   Let $\varphi : \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}$ be locally integrable with respect to Lebesgue measure.
--   For a mesh $h > 0$ let
--
--   $$x_h(s) \;=\; \big(\operatorname{round}(s_i/h)\cdot h\big)_i \qquad (\texttt{stepIndex d h s})$$
--
--   be the nearest point of the scaled lattice $h\mathbb{Z}^d$ to $s$ — equivalently the point of
--   $h\mathbb{Z}^d$ whose half-closed cube $\prod_i[u_i - h/2, u_i + h/2)$ contains $s$ — and let
--
--   $$A_h(u) \;=\; \{\, x \in \mathbb{R}^d : \max_i |x_i - u_i| \le h/2 \,\} \qquad (\texttt{closedCube h u})$$
--
--   be the closed cube of side $h$ at $u$. Then for almost every $s \in \mathbb{R}^d$ the following
--   holds: for every index type $\iota$, every filter $l$ on $\iota$ and every family of meshes
--   $(h_i)_{i \in \iota}$ with $h_i > 0$ for all $i$ and $h_i \to 0$ along $l$,
--
--   $$\frac{1}{\lambda_d\big(A_{h_i}(x_{h_i}(s))\big)}\int_{A_{h_i}(x_{h_i}(s))} \varphi(y)\,dy
--   \;\longrightarrow\; \varphi(s) \qquad \text{along } l,$$
--
--   the left-hand side being the mean value of $\varphi$ over the cube with respect to Lebesgue
--   measure $\lambda_d$ (Lean's `⨍`).
--
--   This is the Lebesgue differentiation theorem along the cubes of a shrinking lattice tiling. The
--   cubes are *not* centred at $s$ — they are the tiles of the lattice, and $s$ may sit anywhere inside
--   its tile, even at a corner — so this is not the plain differentiation theorem for balls; what makes
--   it work is that a cube of side $h$ containing $s$ occupies a fixed fraction of the volume of the
--   ball $\bar{B}_{h\sqrt{d}}(s)$, so the tiles form a Vitali family. It is the tool that lets the step
--   approximation $f_h$ of a function be compared with the function itself pointwise almost everywhere.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The quantifiers are arranged so that the exceptional null set is chosen
--   *before* the family of meshes: a single set of full measure works simultaneously for every index
--   type, every filter and every admissible family $(h_i)$. This is stronger than the statement
--   obtained by fixing one sequence $h_n \to 0$ first, and is what makes the result usable for families
--   indexed by filters other than $\mathbb{N}$.
